+# Desktop performance
+
+This file records what frames cost in this app's browser, what was measured,
+what changed and what it bought. Everything here was measured headlessly with
+`ui.Tester`, which builds real frames (including text shaping, layout and, on
+the CPU, painting) without a window.
+
+## What a frame costs
+
+Immediate mode means the view is built again for every frame, so everything a
+frame computes is paid at frame rate: formatting a row's size, date and kind,
+filling the maps that say where rows showed, asking the netdisk for
+thumbnails, telling uploads from the files a folder lists. A folder of 2 000
+files showed three costs that scale with the folder or with garbage:
+
+1. `rowTops` walked all 2 000 files and built a `map[int64]float32` of them on
+   **every** list frame — 105 KB per frame, 41% of a frame's allocations. That
+   garbage is what brings the collector in: 0.005 GCs per frame before, 0.001
+   after.
+2. `l.chosen()` — a walk of every file plus a slice of those chosen — ran on
+   every browser frame from `filesPage`, `fileActions` and `selectionBar`,
+   even with nothing chosen, and three times over with 100 files chosen
+   (225 KB per frame).
+3. Uploads gathering into a big folder had `arrivals` walk the folder once for
+   **each** uploading file, every frame (`slices.ContainsFunc` over
+   `l.files`): 50 uploads into 2 000 files walked 100 000 entries a frame.
+4. Per visible row, `humanSize`, `stampTime`, `kindLabel` and `typeLabel`
+   reformatted identical strings every frame (about 4 strings and allocations
+   each), `bindListState` made three closures, `rowRects`/`rowsBuilt` were new
+   maps, and the context-menu closure copied the whole `baidu.File` onto the
+   heap.
+
+## Before and after
+
+Measured with `go test ./app -run '^$' -bench 'Benchmark' -benchmem`
+(Intel i5-13400, 16 threads, machine shared with other builds). Wall time is
+the minimum of four paired runs (the files reverted to `HEAD` and swapped back
+between runs, so both sides saw the same load); allocations and `gc/op` are
+exact.
+
+| Benchmark (one frame) | ns/op min before → after | median before → after | B/op before → after | allocs before → after | gc/op before → after |
+|---|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|
+| `BenchmarkFilesList2000` — list of 2 000 files | 568 281 → 487 517 (**−14%**) | 596 → 521 µs (−13%) | 105 553 → 23 130 | 930 → 687 | 0.0052 → 0.0011 |
+| `BenchmarkFilesListSelected` — same, 100 chosen | 726 854 → 601 481 (**−17%**) | 833 → 643 µs (−23%) | 225 649 → 103 804 | 1052 → 801 | 0.0036 → 0.0018 |
+| `BenchmarkFilesListUploads` — 2 000 files, 50 uploads | 821 991 → 516 276 (**−37%**) | 916 → 827 µs (−10%) | 105 642 → 23 224 | 939 → 696 | 0.0012 → ≈0 |
+| `BenchmarkFilesGrid2000` — grid of 2 000 files | 336 169 → 353 000 (noise) | 379 → 367 µs (−3%) | 17 657 → 13 576 | 412 → 352 | 0.0006 → 0.0003 |
+| `BenchmarkFilesListIdle` — the small root folder (control) | ~196 000 → ~195 000 | ~205 → ~208 µs | 18 456 → 16 520 | 296 → 266 | 0.0002 → 0.0002 |
+| `BenchmarkTransfersActive` — 200 tasks going (control) | unchanged | unchanged | 110 016 → 110 016 | 322 → 322 | unchanged |
+| `BenchmarkTransfersDone` — 200 finished tasks (control) | unchanged | unchanged | 145 320 → 145 321 | 407 → 407 | unchanged |
+| `BenchmarkSettings` — the settings page (control) | unchanged | unchanged | 16 479 → 16 479 | 207 → 207 | unchanged |
+
+The wall-time numbers move by up to ±15% from run to run on the shared machine
+— the two transfers benchmarks and the settings page, which no change touched,
+are the controls — so the allocation and `gc/op` columns are the reliable part;
+wall time is given as the minimum and the median of four paired runs, the files
+reverted to `HEAD` and swapped back between runs so both sides saw the same
+load. On a desktop the tester's CPU painting is done by the GPU instead, so the
+share of a frame spent on the Go side is larger than these numbers make it
+look; a grid frame's painting hides its (down 23%) allocation change entirely.
+
+## What changed
+
+Each change is a few lines, commented where it lives, and keeps the behaviour:
+row rectangles, marquee, drag, entrance and glide animations and the striping
+are as they were.
+
+- **`app/browser.go` — no map of every row unless rows move.** `rowTops` is
+  only asked for while a row is coming or going (a file dissolving, a failed
+  upload), where row heights differ; otherwise a row's top is its index times
+  `rowHeight`, which is what the map held. `rowsVary` recognises that case.
+  Before: 105 KB and a 2 000-entry walk per frame; after: neither.
+- **`app/browser.go` — the row maps are kept and cleared.** `rowsBuilt`,
+  `rowsShown` and `rowRects` are rotated between frames instead of allocated
+  anew each one.
+- **`app/browser.go` — the list's callbacks are made once.** `bindListState`
+  used to allocate three closures per frame; they now read `l.files` when the
+  list asks them and are kept on the listing.
+- **`app/files.go` — formatted strings once a file, not once a frame.**
+  `textFor`/`typeFor` keep each row's size, kind, date and type (keyed by
+  fs_id, revalidated by name/size/mtime) in the listing, dropped when the files
+  are read again, and the date re-made when the day changes. The date is the
+  one place `now` matters ("今天"/"昨天"), so an entry remembers the day it was
+  written for.
+- **`app/browser.go` — a choice is not rebuilt to be counted.** The
+  "one file chosen alone" test and the selection bar now ask `l.sel` first
+  instead of `l.chosen()`, so a folder with nothing chosen does not walk every
+  file (and a folder with 100 chosen no longer walks it a third time). The
+  status bar's draft count and the draft row are asked of a flag the browser
+  keeps (`hasDraft`) rather than `l.index(draftID)`, and `placeDraft` — a walk
+  of every file — runs only while a folder is being named or one still shows.
+- **`app/browser.go` — the context menu holds the file by pointer.** The
+  closure used to copy the whole `baidu.File` (three strings and six numbers)
+  onto the heap for every row and tile in view, every frame.
+- **`app/arrivals.go` — the uploads are told apart from a name set.**
+  `listingNames` keeps the names a listing holds in a set (made when the files
+  are read, dropped when a file dissolves out of them) and `arrivals` tests each
+  gathering upload against it: the per-frame cost went from
+  `uploads × files` comparisons to `files` (once) + `uploads`.
+
+## What was measured and left alone
+
+- **Thumbnails (`app/thumbs.go`).** A thumbnail already in memory costs one
+  map lookup per visible row or tile per frame (plus the small one it falls
+  back through) — well under 1 µs per visible tile, and below what a frame's
+  painting costs in the tester, so the scheduler was left as it is: entries are
+  keyed by `(fsid, mtime, size, small)` so the same thumbnail is never asked
+  for twice, the queue is capped at 4 096 and six workers fetch, and a job a
+  frame no longer shows is dropped before it is fetched (its entry is deleted
+  on the main thread through `a.update`). Nothing there showed up in a frame
+  profile of a 2 000-file grid or list.
+
+## Second round: the glide bug, the key walks and the transfers page
+
+Once the frame's own allocations were down, the remaining per-frame work was
+in four places; all four are fixed below.
+
+**The glide bug (`app/landing.go`).** A row whose place moves glides there over
+`motionPanel` (180 ms). The check that starts a glide compared the row's place
+recorded last frame — which was recorded *after* the frame restarted the
+glide — with this frame's progress: as soon as a glide's step per frame came to
+more than `rowHeight/2` (14 DIP, so a shift of five rows or more), the two
+differed by the step and the glide was restarted every frame. Measured on five
+upload rows sorting to the top of a folder: **26 glides still alive and 1 638
+restarts over one second, the view invalidating every frame — a `ui.Tester`
+frame built 20 views, the tester's cap, and would never settle.** A desktop
+kept repainting at full speed and drew the rows short of their places for as
+long as nothing else moved them.
+
+The fix records the rows' *places* in `rowsShown`/`rowsBuilt` rather than where
+each was drawn, so a glide is started by a place moving (and follows it again
+if it moves during the glide), never by its own progress.
+
+**`fileActions`'s walk (`app/ops.go`).** The browser's key handler asked for the
+chosen files — a walk of the folder and a slice of every chosen file — at the
+top of every frame, before it looked at any key. It now asks only after the
+folder keys (path up, new folder, refresh, undo, paste, select all, view
+switch) and only if something is chosen at all. The walk was about 2 µs for
+2 000 files, below the noise of a 500 µs frame; it is kept as a cost that grows
+with the folder and does not depend on a key being pressed.
+
+**The transfers page (`app/transfers.go`).** Every frame split the whole task
+list into the going and the finished (two slices the size of the queue, plus
+their growth), walked it again for the toolbar's counts, and formatted every
+finished row's line again. `transfersState` now keeps those lists and counts
+(going, done, stopped, active, running, down, up, queued, their speed, and a
+finished task's line) made once for each change of the tasks — `gen`, the
+change counter the app already keeps, with the tasks' length and first task
+addressed as well so that a caller replacing the slice without counting a
+change still gets the new lists.
+
+**`rowTops`'s map (`app/landing.go`).** While rows come or go it computed every
+row's top into a fresh `map[int64]float32` of every file, every frame, for as
+long as the animation lasted. It now fills a `[]float32` the listing keeps and
+grows (`listing.tops`), which a frame allocates nothing for.
+
+Measured the same way as the first table: the minimum of three paired runs, the
+five files reverted to their state before the second round and swapped back
+between runs. The `BenchmarkFilesListGathering` benchmark is the five uploads'
+rows arriving at the top of a 2 000-file folder.
+
+| Benchmark (one frame) | ns/op before → after | B/op before → after | allocs before → after |
+|---|--:|--:|--:|
+| `BenchmarkRowTops` — a row-top pass over 2 000 files (per call) | 50 324 → 8 359 (**−83%**) | 73 936 → 0 | 10 → 0 |
+| `BenchmarkTransfersActive` — 200 tasks going | 456 313 → 462 309 (noise; median 538 → 529 µs) | 110 019 → 12 320 (**−89%**) | 322 → 307 |
+| `BenchmarkTransfersDone` — 200 finished tasks | 473 138 → 491 708 (noise; median 521 → 492 µs) | 145 324 → 14 553 (**−90%**) | 407 → 398 |
+| `BenchmarkFilesListGathering` — five upload rows arriving | cannot be measured: a settled frame built 20 views (the tester's cap) | — | — |
+| the same, once the glide settles | 503 362–546 143 ns (503–546 µs) | 24 315 | 725 |
+| `BenchmarkFilesList2000` (control for the shortcut fix) | min 570 812 → 510 721 (noise; median 571 → 566 µs) | 23 130 → 23 146 | 687 → 687 |
+| `BenchmarkFilesListSelected` (control for the shortcut fix) | min 813 766 → 610 690 (noise; median 814 → 662 µs) | 103 807 → 103 822 | 801 → 801 |
+
+The transfers page's allocation drop is the largest of the second round: a queue
+of 200 tasks no longer copies itself into two new slices and formats every
+finished line on every frame (110 KB → 12 KB with a task going, 145 KB → 15 KB
+with them finished; the collector is no longer brought in by a page that shows
+the same queue: `gc/op` 0.0013 → 0 and 0.0017 → 0.0004). Its wall time in the
+tester barely moves because painting the rows and the speed chart is most of
+that frame on the CPU; a desktop draws the same rows on the GPU and pays the Go
+side, which is what these columns measure.
+
+`TestGlideSettles` in `app/bench_test.go` guards the glide fix: five upload rows
+arrive, the tester advances a second, and then the view must not ask for another
+frame and a frame must build exactly one view. It fails on the code before the
+fix (`the view still asks for frames after the rows glided: 26 glides left`) and
+`BenchmarkFilesListGathering` fails there too (`a settled frame builds 20 views,
+want 1`).
+
+## Reproducing
+
+```sh
+export GOMODCACHE=$PWD/.toolchain/gomodcache GOPATH=$PWD/.toolchain/gopath GOCACHE=$PWD/.gocache GOFLAGS=-mod=mod
+go test ./app -run '^$' -bench 'Benchmark' -benchmem -count=3
+```
+
+Each benchmark signs in to the fake netdisk, opens a folder of 2 000 files
+(none with a thumbnail, so a frame does not fetch images), settles the one-shot
+animations with `tt.Advance(time.Second)`, checks that a settled `tt.Frame()`
+builds exactly one view (`builds/frame` in the output: the tester's clock is
+frozen during `Frame()`, so an animation in flight always asks for another
+frame and the tester builds up to 20), and then times frames at a fixed clock.
+
+To find a hot spot:
+
+```sh
+go test ./app -run '^$' -bench 'BenchmarkFilesList2000$' -benchtime=5000x \
+  -cpuprofile=/tmp/list.cpu -memprofile=/tmp/list.mem
+go tool pprof -top -sample_index=alloc_space /tmp/list.mem
+go tool pprof -top /tmp/list.cpu
+```
+
+Absolute times are a `ui.Tester`'s: it draws on the CPU (the rasterizer and the
+text shaper are what a 2 000-file list spends most of its frame on) while a
+desktop draws on the GPU. The same view, window size, font and effects are built
+on both sides of a comparison, which is what the numbers above compare.
